\title{API Reference}

\p{
  Top-level \code{import baux} exposes a lazy-loaded namespace (\code{__getattr__}) resolving functional state transitions, composable pipeline combinators, manifold domain charts, standalone Epistemic Nearest Neighbor (\code{ENN}) surrogates, and Rust-backed primitives on demand without eagerly importing optional array or compiler frameworks. Across all entrypoints, flat parameter matrices \code{x} have shape \code{(N, D)} (\code{float64}) inside natural domain bounds \code{[low, high]}, while objective arrays \code{y} (maximization convention) and optional heteroscedastic observation variances \code{y_var} have shape \code{(N,)} for scalar targets or \code{(N, M)} (\code{float64}) for multi-output objectives. Pure functional transitions (\code{baux.init}, \code{baux.ask}, \code{baux.tell}, \code{baux.enn_init}, \code{baux.enn_append}) operate on immutable, content-addressed copy-on-write snapshots ([\code{OptimizerState}](#baux.OptimizerState), [\code{ENNState}](#baux.ENNState)) whose array properties return read-only views (\code{writeable=False}), whereas [\code{Optimizer}](#baux.Optimizer) and [\code{ENN}](#baux.ENN) provide stateful in-place harnesses over the same native execution engine.
}

\<div>[id]{baux-api-filter}{}

