  \<div>[id]{baux.Telemetry}[class]{api-entry}[data-symbol]{baux.Telemetry}[data-kind]{class}[data-module]{baux.bo}{
    \<div>[class]{api-entry-header}{
      \<span>[class]{api-entry-title}{[\code{baux.Telemetry}](#baux.Telemetry)}
      \<span>[class]{api-kind-badge}{class}
    }
    \pre{
baux.Telemetry(
    dt_fit: float,
    dt_sel: float,
    dt_gen: float = 0.0,
    dt_tell: float = 0.0,
    num_candidates: int = 0,
)
    }
    \p{Frozen slotted dataclass reporting native wall-clock phase durations (in seconds) and candidate pool cardinality for the latest optimization cycle.}
    \<dl>[class]{api-spec-list}{
      \<dt>{Attributes}
      \<dd>{
        \ul{
          \li{\code{dt_fit} (\em{float}) — Surrogate hyperparameter fit duration in seconds.}
          \li{\code{dt_sel} (\em{float}) — Acquisition evaluation and arm selection duration in seconds.}
          \li{\code{dt_gen} (\em{float}, default \code{0.0}) — Candidate pool generation duration in seconds.}
          \li{\code{dt_tell} (\em{float}, default \code{0.0}) — Observation ingestion duration in seconds.}
          \li{\code{num_candidates} (\em{int}, default \code{0}) — Number of candidate points generated during \code{ask}.}
        }
      }
    }
  }

  \<div>[id]{baux.TellPredictionError}[class]{api-entry}[data-symbol]{baux.TellPredictionError}[data-kind]{class}[data-module]{baux.bo}{
    \<div>[class]{api-entry-header}{
      \<span>[class]{api-entry-title}{[\code{baux.TellPredictionError}](#baux.TellPredictionError)}
      \<span>[class]{api-kind-badge}{class}
    }
    \pre{
class baux.TellPredictionError(RuntimeError)
    }
    \p{Exception raised when [\code{baux.tell}](#baux.tell), [\code{baux.import_observations}](#baux.import_observations), \code{Optimizer.tell}, or \code{Optimizer.import_observations} commits observations to the native store, but the subsequent \code{posterior_mu(x)} evaluation fails.}
    \<dl>[class]{api-spec-list}{
      \<dt>{Attributes}
      \<dd>{
        \ul{
          \li{\code{observations_accepted} (\em{bool}) — Class and instance invariant flag always equal to \code{True}.}
        }
      }
      \<dt>{Notes}
      \<dd>{Because \code{observations_accepted == True} guarantees that the native optimizer has already committed \code{(x, y)} and retired any corresponding pending batch, callers handling \code{TellPredictionError} must not re-invoke \code{tell(x, y)}; recover predictive means by calling [\code{baux.posterior_mu}](#baux.posterior_mu) (or \code{Optimizer.posterior_mu}) directly.}
    }
  }

  \<div>[id]{baux.optuna.Sampler}[class]{api-entry}[data-symbol]{baux.optuna.Sampler}[data-kind]{class}[data-module]{baux.optuna}{
    \<div>[class]{api-entry-header}{
      \<span>[class]{api-entry-title}{[\code{baux.optuna.Sampler}](#baux.optuna.Sampler)}
      \<span>[class]{api-kind-badge}{class}
    }
    \pre{
baux.optuna.Sampler(
    space: Mapping[str, FloatDistribution],
    *,
    config: OptimizerConfig | None = None,
    seed: int | None = None,
)
    }
    \p{Subclass of \code{optuna.samplers.BaseSampler} routing fixed continuous single-objective Optuna studies through a stateful BAUX [\code{Optimizer}](#baux.Optimizer). Imported via \code{import baux.optuna} (requires optional \code{optuna} package).}
    \<dl>[class]{api-spec-list}{
      \<dt>{Parameters}
      \<dd>{
        \ul{
          \li{\code{space} (\em{Mapping[str, optuna.distributions.FloatDistribution]}) — Non-empty mapping of parameter names to continuous \code{FloatDistribution} bounds (\code{step} must be \code{None}; \code{log=True} parameters transform via \code{log} / \code{exp}).}
          \li{\code{config} (\em{OptimizerConfig | None}, keyword-only, default \code{None}) — Optional single-region [\code{OptimizerConfig}](#baux.OptimizerConfig) (defaults to \code{OptimizerConfig()}; \code{RegionalSearchPolicy} raises \code{ValueError}).}
          \li{\code{seed} (\em{int | None}, keyword-only, default \code{None}) — Optional deterministic integer seed (\code{None} draws fresh entropy).}
        }
      }
      \<dt>{Methods}
      \<dd>{
        \ul{
          \li{\code{infer_relative_search_space(study: Study, trial: FrozenTrial) -> dict[str, BaseDistribution]} — Return the fixed declared \code{FloatDistribution} mapping.}
          \li{\code{sample_relative(study: Study, trial: FrozenTrial, search_space: dict[str, BaseDistribution]) -> dict[str, Any]} — Synchronize newly finished \code{COMPLETE} and valued \code{PRUNED} trials (negating \code{MINIMIZE} studies to BAUX maximization convention) and propose the next arm.}
          \li{\code{sample_independent(study: Study, trial: FrozenTrial, param_name: str, param_distribution: BaseDistribution) -> Any} — Unconditionally raises \code{ValueError} to reject dynamic, discrete, or conditional parameters instead of silently falling back to independent random sampling.}
          \li{\code{reseed_rng() -> None} — Unconditionally raises \code{RuntimeError} to reject multi-worker \code{study.optimize(n_jobs != 1)} on a shared stateful sampler.}
        }
      }
      \<dt>{Notes}
      \<dd>{Each \code{Sampler} binds to at most one single-objective \code{Study} (reusing across studies or passing multi-objective \code{study.directions} raises \code{ValueError}). Concurrent \code{RUNNING} trials are skipped cleanly without fabricating fantasy objective values.}
    }
  }

  \<div>[id]{baux.experimental.program}[class]{api-entry}[data-symbol]{baux.experimental.program}[data-kind]{function}[data-module]{baux.experimental.program}{
    \<div>[class]{api-entry-header}{
      \<span>[class]{api-entry-title}{[\code{baux.experimental.program}](#baux.experimental.program)}
      \<span>[class]{api-kind-badge}{module}
    }
    \pre{
baux.experimental.program.compile(
    source: str | Callable[..., Any],
    inputs: Any,
    outputs: Any = None,
    *,
    backend: Literal["iree", "tvm", "xla"] = "iree",
    device: str = "cpu",
    entry: str = "main",
) -> Executable
    }
    \p{Experimental compiler and resident-buffer execution interface lowering StableHLO MLIR modules or JAX-traced Python callables onto IREE, Apache TVM Relax, or XLA runtimes. Imported explicitly via \code{from baux.experimental import program} (requires optional \code{compiler} dependencies).}
    \<dl>[class]{api-spec-list}{
      \<dt>{Parameters}
      \<dd>{
        \ul{
          \li{\code{source} (\em{str | Callable[..., Any]}) — StableHLO MLIR module string or Python callable lowered via \code{jax.jit(...).lower(*example_args).compiler_ir(dialect="stablehlo")}.}
          \li{\code{inputs} (\em{Any}) — Nested OpTree of sample arrays or \code{TensorSpec(shape: tuple[int, ...], dtype: str = "float32")} descriptors defining argument tree layout and static shapes.}
          \li{\code{outputs} (\em{Any}, default \code{None}) — Optional output OpTree specification required when \code{source} is raw MLIR text returning structured containers.}
          \li{\code{backend} (\em{Literal["iree", "tvm", "xla"]}, keyword-only, default \code{"iree"}) — Target compiler stack (\code{"iree"} VMFB, \code{"tvm"} StableHLO-to-Relax VM, or \code{"xla"} \code{jax.jit}).}
          \li{\code{device} (\em{str}, keyword-only, default \code{"cpu"}) — Execution target (\code{"cpu"}, \code{"metal"}, \code{"vulkan"}, or \code{"cuda"}).}
          \li{\code{entry} (\em{str}, keyword-only, default \code{"main"}) — Entrypoint function name inside the compiled module.}
        }
      }
      \<dt>{Attributes}
      \<dd>{
        \ul{
          \li{\code{Executable} (\em{class}) — Compiled session handle returned by \code{compile(...)}, providing \code{put(tree)} (host-to-device OpTree transfer returning \code{Buffer} leaves), \code{get(tree)} (explicit device-to-host NumPy readback), synchronous \code{__call__(tree)}, and non-blocking \code{submit(tree) -> Invocation}.}
          \li{\code{Buffer} (\em{class}) — Opaque device-resident array handle exposing \code{shape: tuple[int, ...]} and \code{dtype: str}; \code{__array__} raises \code{TypeError} to prevent accidental implicit host transfers.}
          \li{\code{Invocation} (\em{class}) — Asynchronous execution future exposing \code{ready() -> bool}, \code{wait() -> Any}, and \code{__await__()}.}
          \li{\code{TensorSpec(shape: tuple[int, ...], dtype: str = "float32")} (\em{frozen dataclass}) — Shape/dtype placeholder leaf for \code{compile}.}
          \li{\code{register(cls: type) -> type} (\em{function}) — Decorator registering a Python class as an OpTree container in namespace \code{"baux.program"}.}
          \li{\code{ResidentModel}, \code{ModelInvocation}, \code{ModelOutput} (\em{classes}) — Device-resident causal forward-loss harness keeping base transformer weights pinned on device across zeroth-order \code{put_perturbation}, \code{submit_perturbation}, and \code{forward_loss} evaluations.}
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

\subtree{
  \title{Runtime Capabilities & Platform Targets}

  \<table>[class]{api-summary-table}{
    \<thead>{
      \<tr>{
        \<th>{Symbol}
        \<th>{Summary}
      }
    }
    \<tbody>{
      \<tr>[data-symbol]{baux.capabilities}[data-kind]{function}[data-module]{baux.runtime}{
        \<td>{[\code{baux.capabilities}](#baux.capabilities)}
        \<td>{Inspect compiled and runtime-probed backend capabilities across CPU, CUDA, Metal, and OpenCL.}
      }
      \<tr>[data-symbol]{baux.BackendCapabilities}[data-kind]{class}[data-module]{baux.runtime}{
        \<td>{[\code{baux.BackendCapabilities}](#baux.BackendCapabilities)}
        \<td>{Frozen capability record detailing direct, fallback, host-only, and missing operations per backend.}
      }
      \<tr>[data-symbol]{baux.Device}[data-kind]{class}[data-module]{baux.runtime}{
        \<td>{[\code{baux.Device}](#baux.Device)}
        \<td>{Native Rust accelerator handle (\code{"auto"}, \code{"cpu"}, \code{"metal"}, \code{"cuda"}) and Metal memory inspector.}
      }
    }
  }

  \<div>[id]{baux.capabilities}[class]{api-entry}[data-symbol]{baux.capabilities}[data-kind]{function}[data-module]{baux.runtime}{
    \<div>[class]{api-entry-header}{
      \<span>[class]{api-entry-title}{[\code{baux.capabilities}](#baux.capabilities)}
      \<span>[class]{api-kind-badge}{function}
    }
    \pre{
baux.capabilities(*, probe: bool = False) -> tuple[BackendCapabilities, ...]
    }
    \p{Return a 4-tuple of [\code{BackendCapabilities}](#baux.BackendCapabilities) descriptors ordered \code{("cpu", "cuda", "metal", "opencl")}.}
    \<dl>[class]{api-spec-list}{
      \<dt>{Parameters}
      \<dd>{
        \ul{
          \li{\code{probe} (\em{bool}, keyword-only, default \code{False}) — When \code{False}, reports compile-time feature inclusion without touching GPU drivers (\code{available=None} on compiled accelerators, \code{available=True} on \code{"cpu"}). When \code{True}, actively probes hardware devices and populates \code{available: bool} and diagnostic \code{reason}.}
        }
      }
      \<dt>{Returns}
      \<dd>{\code{tuple[BackendCapabilities, ...]} — Capability records for CPU, CUDA, Metal, and OpenCL.}
    }
  }

  \<div>[id]{baux.BackendCapabilities}[class]{api-entry}[data-symbol]{baux.BackendCapabilities}[data-kind]{class}[data-module]{baux.runtime}{
    \<div>[class]{api-entry-header}{
      \<span>[class]{api-entry-title}{[\code{baux.BackendCapabilities}](#baux.BackendCapabilities)}
      \<span>[class]{api-kind-badge}{class}
    }
    \pre{
baux.BackendCapabilities(
    name: Literal["cpu", "cuda", "metal", "opencl"],
    compiled: bool,
    available: bool | None,
    reason: str | None,
    direct_operations: tuple[str, ...],
    fallback_operations: tuple[str, ...],
    missing_operations: tuple[str, ...],
    host_operations: tuple[str, ...],
    array_frameworks: tuple[str, ...],
)
    }
    \p{Frozen slotted dataclass describing compilation status, runtime hardware availability, operation coverage, and supported zero-copy DLPack array frameworks for one execution backend.}
    \<dl>[class]{api-spec-list}{
      \<dt>{Attributes}
      \<dd>{
        \ul{
          \li{\code{name} (\em{Literal["cpu", "cuda", "metal", "opencl"]}) — Canonical backend identifier.}
          \li{\code{compiled} (\em{bool}) — Whether the active wheel binary was compiled with support for this backend.}
          \li{\code{available} (\em{bool | None}) — Hardware probe outcome (\code{True} or \code{False} when \code{probe=True} or \code{compiled=False}; \code{None} for unprobed compiled GPU backends).}
          \li{\code{reason} (\em{str | None}) — Human-readable explanation when \code{compiled=False} or \code{available=False}, else \code{None}.}
          \li{\code{direct_operations} (\em{tuple[str, ...]}) — Operations executed natively on the backend (e.g., \code{"distance"}, \code{"knn_search"}, \code{"device_posterior"}, \code{"posterior_draw"}, \code{"resident_bf16_search"}).}
          \li{\code{fallback_operations} (\em{tuple[str, ...]}) — Operations routed through host fallback paths when invoked on this backend.}
          \li{\code{missing_operations} (\em{tuple[str, ...]}) — Operations unsupported on this backend (such as \code{"device_posterior"} on \code{"opencl"}).}
          \li{\code{host_operations} (\em{tuple[str, ...]}) — Operations intentionally executed in \code{f64} on the host CPU (e.g., \code{"exact_pool_certification"}, \code{"loo_fit"}, \code{"turbo_step"}, \code{"morbo_pareto"}).}
          \li{\code{array_frameworks} (\em{tuple[str, ...]}) — Compatible array ecosystems (\code{("numpy",)} on CPU, \code{("torch_cuda", "jax_cuda")} on CUDA, \code{("torch_mps",)} on Metal).}
        }
      }
    }
  }

  \<div>[id]{baux.Device}[class]{api-entry}[data-symbol]{baux.Device}[data-kind]{class}[data-module]{baux.runtime}{
    \<div>[class]{api-entry-header}{
      \<span>[class]{api-entry-title}{[\code{baux.Device}](#baux.Device)}
      \<span>[class]{api-kind-badge}{class}
    }
    \pre{
baux.Device(name: str = "auto") -> Device
    }
    \p{Native Rust accelerator selector resolving \code{"auto"}, \code{"cpu"}, \code{"metal"}, or \code{"cuda"} execution contexts and exposing Apple Metal unified-memory limits.}
    \<dl>[class]{api-spec-list}{
      \<dt>{Attributes}
      \<dd>{
        \ul{
          \li{\code{kind} (\em{str}) — Resolved backend string (\code{"cpu"}, \code{"metal"}, or \code{"cuda"}).}
          \li{\code{is_cpu} (\em{bool}) — \code{True} iff \code{kind == "cpu"}.}
          \li{\code{is_metal} (\em{bool}) — \code{True} iff \code{kind == "metal"}.}
          \li{\code{is_cuda} (\em{bool}) — \code{True} iff \code{kind == "cuda"}.}
        }
      }
      \<dt>{Methods}
      \<dd>{
        \ul{
          \li{\code{Device.auto() -> Device} — Select the highest-throughput available accelerator, falling back to \code{"cpu"}.}
          \li{\code{Device.cpu() -> Device} — Explicitly select the host CPU \code{f64} backend.}
          \li{\code{Device.metal() -> Device} — Explicitly select the Apple Metal GPU backend (raises \code{ValueError} if unavailable).}
          \li{\code{Device.cuda() -> Device} — Explicitly select the NVIDIA CUDA GPU backend (raises \code{ValueError} if unavailable).}
          \li{\code{memory_info() -> dict[str, Any]} — Return Metal device telemetry keys \code{"name"} (\code{str}), \code{"recommended_working_set_bytes"} (\code{int}), \code{"allocated_bytes"} (\code{int}), and \code{"max_buffer_bytes"} (\code{int}); raises \code{ValueError} on \code{"cpu"}.}
        }
      }
    }
  }

  \<div>[id]{hardware-backends}{
    \p{\strong{Hardware Backends & Wheel Targets.} BAUX ships precompiled PyO3/maturin wheels for CPython 3.12–3.15 (\code{cp312}–\code{cp315}) alongside source distributions. All surrogate hyperparameter fits, exact candidate pool certification, and TuRBO/MORBO trust-region controllers retain IEEE-754 \code{float64} host precision even when neighbor sweeps and posterior draws run on GPU accelerators.}
    \<table>{
      \<thead>{
        \<tr>{
          \<th>{Platform Target}
          \<th>{Architecture}
          \<th>{Compiled Kernels & Precision}
          \<th>{Wheel Tags (Python 3.12–3.15)}
        }
      }
      \<tbody>{
        \<tr>{
          \<td>{\strong{macOS 11.0+ (Metal + CPU)}}
          \<td>{\code{arm64}}
          \<td>{Custom Metal Shading Language (\code{MSL}) compute shaders (\code{f32}/\code{bf16} distance sweeps, zero-copy Torch MPS DLPack \code{device_posterior}, \code{f32} function draws, resident \code{bf16} search) paired with Apple Silicon NEON / portable \code{f64} CPU solvers and exact certification.}
          \<td>{\code{cp312}–\code{cp315} \code{macosx_11_0_arm64}}
        }
        \<tr>{
          \<td>{\strong{Linux CUDA}}
          \<td>{\code{x86_64}}
          \<td>{NVIDIA CUDA (\code{sm_75+} Turing and newer) shared-memory tiled distance kernels, cuVS index integration, Box–Muller \code{f64} coherent draws, zero-copy Torch/JAX CUDA DLPack \code{device_posterior}, and resident \code{bf16} search paired with \code{f64} host solvers.}
          \<td>{\code{cp312}–\code{cp315} \code{manylinux_2_28_x86_64} (\code{+cuda75} local variant)}
        }
        \<tr>{
          \<td>{\strong{CPU Baseline}}
          \<td>{\code{x86_64} / \code{aarch64}}
          \<td>{Portable Rust \code{core::simd::f64x4} SIMD and scalar \code{f64} exact, USearch HNSW, and disk-backed BPANN neighbor indices, SplitMix64/ChaCha12 PRNG streams, and GIL-free multi-threaded \code{pmap} execution.}
          \<td>{\code{cp312}–\code{cp315} \code{manylinux_2_28_x86_64}, \code{manylinux_2_28_aarch64}, \code{macosx_11_0_arm64}, \code{sdist}}
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
